#   Comments + Keyword Clarification

#    Anton, Calculus, 2.6.78
# DESCRIPTION
# Find the derivative of a function using the product rule and chain rule

# ENDDESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Differentiation)
## DBsection(Chain rule (without trigonometric functions))
## Institution(John Wiley & Sons)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Anton')
## EditionText1('9')
## Section1('2.6')
## Problem1('78')
## KEYWORDS('Derivative', 'Chain rule')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");
Context()->variables->add(u => 'Real');

$p   = random(2, 9, 1);
$pm1 = $p - 1;

$Df1 = Formula(" 1/2*x^($pm1)")->reduce;
$xp  = Formula(" x^($pm1)");
if ($p == 2) { $xp = Formula(" x "); }

BEGIN_PGML
Find [`f'(x^2)`] if [` \frac{d}{dx}[f(x^2)] = x^[$p]`].

[`f'(x^2) =`] [_]{$Df1}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Let [`u=x^2`]. Then [` \frac{du}{dx} = 2x`], and by the chain rule

 [` x^[$p] = \frac{d}{dx}[f(x^2)]=\frac{d}{dx}[f(u)] = \frac{df}{du}\frac{du}{dx}=2x f'(u)`]

Hence,
 [`f'(u)  = \frac{1}{2} [$xp]`]

Replacing [`u`] by [`x^2`]  we get

[``f'(x^2)  = \frac{1}{2} [$xp]``]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
